Well, actually strawberry won.

00:00:56.327 --> 00:01:01.201
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But that's okay.

00:01:01.201 --> 00:01:06.720
We at the GSB love to be outliers,
so it's okay, I understand.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.538
Well, and then here's some
behind the scenes footage.

00:01:09.538 --> 00:01:12.062
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; If you'd like to use these as

00:01:12.062 --> 00:01:13.704
marketing materials, feel free.

00:01:13.704 --> 00:01:18.284
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In preparation for our conversation,

00:01:18.284 --> 00:01:22.128
I learned that Chobani can be
loosely translated to shepherd.

00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:24.820
Can you tell us about
the significance there?

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:32.470
&gt;&gt; Sure, and your background is Greek, as
I understood just now which is beautiful.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.442
And I don't know if you speak Greek but
it also sounds like Chobani but

00:01:36.442 --> 00:01:37.480
I won't kill it.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:43.520
It's like [FOREIGN] something like
this and in Turkish it's coban.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.238
And by the way,
I want to say Madhava to my Turkish

00:01:51.238 --> 00:01:55.325
brothers and sisters, they're here.

00:01:55.325 --> 00:01:58.989
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And, of course, all of you,

00:01:58.989 --> 00:02:02.028
I'm just homesick, but

00:02:02.028 --> 00:02:07.395
then I grew up on
the Eastern part of Turkey.

00:02:07.395 --> 00:02:13.798
Northeast and Nomad, a tribal life.

00:02:13.798 --> 00:02:19.030
So my earliest memories are with
shepherds and coban means shepherds.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.759
So my earliest memories are all shepherds,
shepherd stories.

00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:27.102
Interaction with shepherds,
family members being shepherds.

00:02:27.102 --> 00:02:33.600
I was too little but being eager to go
with them to the caring for the animals.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.284
So when I was looking for name,

00:02:36.284 --> 00:02:41.940
I was traveling from Chicago to Madison,
Wisconsin.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.436
And the reason for that is I drove from
that road to go to this old equipment

00:02:46.436 --> 00:02:48.860
dealership in Madison, Wisconsin.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:53.531
So I can buy this separator when
it's essential to make Greek

00:02:53.531 --> 00:02:56.745
yogurt to separate liquid from the mass.

00:02:56.745 --> 00:03:01.417
[COUGH] And in that drive, I don't know
if any of you have done that drive.

00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:05.069
It's a very boring drive,
it's a long drive.

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:06.483
Not much radios work, and

00:03:06.483 --> 00:03:10.593
it's 2006, I don't know if I was bored or
if it was the Christian radio I

00:03:10.593 --> 00:03:14.270
was listening that they were talking
about shepherds all the time.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.248
I came up with the name Chobani at that
drive and when I came back to Chicago and

00:03:20.248 --> 00:03:25.680
I looked it up and chobani.com was
available and that's how we came up.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.610
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:30.286
Well, we've learned in a lot of our
classes and case studies the importance of

00:03:30.286 --> 00:03:34.350
a brand connection and that brand history
and you obviously have a fantastic one.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:39.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I really be, I started
Shepherds Give foundation day one.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.599
A lot of people don't
know what meanings of it.

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:44.740
Inside the company, people do know.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.356
But they had significance for me was okay,

00:03:50.356 --> 00:03:55.955
Chobani sounds really nice,
as a sound, it sounds nice.

00:03:55.955 --> 00:04:00.863
But then the emotional,
the weight of that word for

00:04:00.863 --> 00:04:05.543
me was,
you look at it throughout the history and

00:04:05.543 --> 00:04:09.101
we can call it the new shepherds now.

00:04:09.101 --> 00:04:15.684
But you look at throughout the generations
of humankind, we look up to shepherds and

00:04:15.684 --> 00:04:20.785
represented pureness,
it represented unconditional giving,

00:04:20.785 --> 00:04:24.510
it represented humanity,
safety and wisdom.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.463
And I have seen those in my own
experience as a growing up.

00:04:30.463 --> 00:04:34.256
So I thought,
if I can bring the elements of those,

00:04:34.256 --> 00:04:37.257
right from the beginning with the name,

00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:42.218
into this company that I'm launching
it would be a great success.

00:04:42.218 --> 00:04:44.168
And I have no idea how long would I go.

00:04:44.168 --> 00:04:46.785
I had no idea how far I would make it or

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:50.330
if I would be able to put
elements of it in there.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:52.296
But my personal connections, but

00:04:52.296 --> 00:04:55.385
also that was the idea that
Chobani worked with well.

00:04:55.385 --> 00:04:59.476
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, well, it all started
on a small farm in Turkey, but

00:04:59.476 --> 00:05:03.650
now obviously Chobani has
found incredible success.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.588
There are countless business ideas and
aspiring founders out there,

00:05:07.588 --> 00:05:10.970
who are all trying to
create the next big thing.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:15.326
In your opinion, what are some of the key
magical ingredients that help create

00:05:15.326 --> 00:05:17.438
a breakthrough company that scales?

00:05:20.022 --> 00:05:20.602
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:05:25.773 --> 00:05:29.986
And, I have to be very honest,

00:05:29.986 --> 00:05:35.010
I did not have a previous knowledge or

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.585
experience of business.

00:05:38.585 --> 00:05:42.438
So, my earlier expense is as I
said the shepherds later on,

00:05:42.438 --> 00:05:47.218
I went to university in Ankara,
became an activist, started publishing

00:05:47.218 --> 00:05:51.938
newspapers and got into trouble with
the government and I had to get out.

00:05:51.938 --> 00:05:53.941
Some experience with the police
station but then out.

00:05:53.941 --> 00:05:55.799
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:55.799 --> 00:05:57.507
&gt;&gt; And then I found myself in upstate

00:05:57.507 --> 00:05:58.917
New York working in a farm.

00:05:58.917 --> 00:06:03.023
So basically,
I was home again in upstate New York and

00:06:03.023 --> 00:06:07.585
not necessarily know what I was
going to do with my life, and

00:06:07.585 --> 00:06:12.809
there was not a massive urgency of
what I'm going to do with my life.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:19.116
One thing that I can tell about myself,
and not in a bragging way is,

00:06:19.116 --> 00:06:21.620
I was never nervous about life.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.371
Not that I had anything about it, but

00:06:23.371 --> 00:06:26.835
I didn't have this urgency of,
I have to do this, or I have to do this.

00:06:26.835 --> 00:06:31.790
I always had, growing up,
being up into those Muslim mountains

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:36.285
where we grow up as nomads,
I was always wondering what was

00:06:36.285 --> 00:06:41.810
behind the tallest mountain that
we could see from our tents.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:46.280
And I had that curiosity that,
what would be beyond those mountains?

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:51.178
So, my journey was always, I wonder
what life is going to be bringing,

00:06:51.178 --> 00:06:52.902
so, I'm just going to go.

00:06:52.902 --> 00:06:56.620
And I found out later on
that I was never rigid.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.218
Like I'd never made plans that
would stop me from changing it,

00:07:01.218 --> 00:07:05.190
going this direction or that direction.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.748
I was in a city trying
to learn English and

00:07:07.748 --> 00:07:11.740
teacher said I had a farm upstate
New York, I went up there.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:12.760
She said, Do you want to stay here?

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.654
I said sure, then I stay.

00:07:14.654 --> 00:07:16.926
And I became a farmer for
a year and a half.

00:07:16.926 --> 00:07:21.890
And then somebody said there is
a economic development side that they

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.528
support businesses.

00:07:23.528 --> 00:07:25.395
And I went there and they said,
Do you want to make cheese?

00:07:25.395 --> 00:07:26.966
I said, yeah,
I'd like to make some cheese.

00:07:26.966 --> 00:07:32.258
[LAUGH] And then later on I saw an ad and
said, fully equipped yogurt plant for

00:07:32.258 --> 00:07:35.286
sale, and
I said I'll go take a look at it.

00:07:35.286 --> 00:07:37.237
That evening and
I went to look at the plans.

00:07:37.237 --> 00:07:40.792
Right then I said, you know what,
I'll buy this place.

00:07:40.792 --> 00:07:43.621
Despite the fact everybody
says you don't have money,

00:07:43.621 --> 00:07:45.676
you have no idea what you're thinking.

00:07:45.676 --> 00:07:50.012
There's a big companies leaving,
but I was just curious,

00:07:50.012 --> 00:07:54.624
what would it be like from that
emotion that I had in that plant?

00:07:54.624 --> 00:08:01.494
So I'm not in a position to say
What makes our businesses to scale?

00:08:01.494 --> 00:08:06.502
Because I've seen enormous, enormous
businesses in front of my eye to start and

00:08:06.502 --> 00:08:08.797
grow, became extremely powerful.

00:08:08.797 --> 00:08:12.150
But I can tell you what
makes joy in business.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:15.671
If you're a founder, and

00:08:15.671 --> 00:08:21.927
if you are the starting
person in the journey.

00:08:21.927 --> 00:08:27.572
I can tell you that you could
have enormous amount of

00:08:27.572 --> 00:08:33.627
experiences through this and
for me it is tremendous.

00:08:33.627 --> 00:08:37.458
I'm a kid from the shepherd's,
I traveled 1000 years,

00:08:37.458 --> 00:08:39.896
I feel like I traveled 1000 years.

00:08:39.896 --> 00:08:44.845
If you look at my early days, I'm in a
tribe where tradition has not changed for

00:08:44.845 --> 00:08:49.883
hundreds of hundreds of years now it's
changed, shepherds have cell phones.

00:08:49.883 --> 00:08:52.151
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But back then when I left,

00:08:52.151 --> 00:08:54.932
it was the same way they made the butter.

00:08:54.932 --> 00:08:56.933
It was the same way they did the weddings.

00:08:56.933 --> 00:08:58.684
It was the same way they grieve.

00:08:58.684 --> 00:09:03.316
It was the same way they interacted
socially with each other.

00:09:03.316 --> 00:09:07.554
It had not had changed so dramatically
only time that they would go see somewhere

00:09:07.554 --> 00:09:11.238
outside of the town when we get sick or
they went to military service.

00:09:11.238 --> 00:09:13.297
But outside of that it was the same thing.

00:09:13.297 --> 00:09:17.916
So for me it's been an amazing journey and

00:09:17.916 --> 00:09:21.616
I had to adjust as I went through.

00:09:21.616 --> 00:09:26.617
But at the true joy,
true power of business comes when

00:09:26.617 --> 00:09:31.631
you see that it has a direct
impact on people's life.

00:09:31.631 --> 00:09:37.148
And as it gets successful and
bigger It becomes extremely powerful,

00:09:37.148 --> 00:09:41.060
and this I had no idea
about this until I started.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:46.015
Now, what I can tell you on that one,
is if you want to

00:09:46.015 --> 00:09:50.866
make business joyful, but
also very successful,

00:09:50.866 --> 00:09:55.630
it has to be embedded very,
very early on day one.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:01.827
And it truly needs to be embedded
early on, and then becomes magical.

00:10:01.827 --> 00:10:06.372
But we just don't know how
things comes into life,

00:10:06.372 --> 00:10:10.397
you just don't know how
you find solutions.

00:10:10.397 --> 00:10:14.277
People will give you credits
99% of it is not you.

00:10:14.277 --> 00:10:18.127
It's the environment that you created or
you've been part of to be created,

00:10:18.127 --> 00:10:21.516
that inputs come from left and
right ideas come from left and right.

00:10:21.516 --> 00:10:25.047
People become heroes,
people become solution makers.

00:10:25.047 --> 00:10:28.236
And it becomes this
magical environment and

00:10:28.236 --> 00:10:31.433
I always said It's elevated environment.

00:10:31.433 --> 00:10:36.208
So how do you make
an elevated environment?

00:10:36.208 --> 00:10:39.716
Yeah, money is good, and
you can do a lot of things with it, and

00:10:39.716 --> 00:10:41.382
people get excited about it.

00:10:41.382 --> 00:10:46.688
The people I hired that they let go
in the factory in that old factory,

00:10:46.688 --> 00:10:49.341
that the old factory old company,

00:10:49.341 --> 00:10:53.478
they just fire them,
left them behind and just left.

00:10:53.478 --> 00:10:57.876
This is another topic that you
could talk about for hours.

00:10:57.876 --> 00:11:01.744
Those same companies
are going to be millionaires.

00:11:01.744 --> 00:11:02.928
Maybe lawyer said I shouldn't be

00:11:02.928 --> 00:11:03.493
saying this-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:03.493 --> 00:11:05.870
&gt;&gt; But the factory workers, right?

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:10.429
But I couldn't motivate them,
if I told them work with me,

00:11:10.429 --> 00:11:13.173
I'm going to make you a millionaire.

00:11:13.173 --> 00:11:15.690
Maybe, but not that great.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:19.347
But if you create an environment and
say, these people left you and

00:11:19.347 --> 00:11:22.940
these people left us,
these people left this factory children,

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:27.279
this community, this farmers, and
I've seen this happen where I come from.

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:31.565
This is the responsibility of
businesses happening everywhere.

00:11:31.565 --> 00:11:35.689
And there is an answer to this we can
go out and scream and protest and

00:11:35.689 --> 00:11:37.175
all that kind of stuff.

00:11:37.175 --> 00:11:42.005
Or even though we don't have money,
even though we don't have experience, even

00:11:42.005 --> 00:11:46.994
though we don't have network, even though
we don't have that kind of capability.

00:11:46.994 --> 00:11:51.074
But we can put ourselves together
in this tiny little town,

00:11:51.074 --> 00:11:56.049
in this very old factory, and we could
dream that this factory can become

00:11:56.049 --> 00:12:00.148
one of the biggest factory in
this country next five years.

00:12:00.148 --> 00:12:03.200
And we could dream that we could be
number one brand in this country and

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.623
we can kick those people ass
who closed this factory and

00:12:05.623 --> 00:12:07.379
we can give the answer to them this way.

00:12:07.379 --> 00:12:11.146
And you can talk to pure poor
factory workers that way and

00:12:11.146 --> 00:12:16.132
if you truly act upon it, you can make
them believe as much as you believe.

00:12:16.132 --> 00:12:18.074
And then magic starts happening.

00:12:18.074 --> 00:12:23.048
Now in that moment, if you say
physically this is not possible.

00:12:23.048 --> 00:12:26.832
Because in order for you to be
a number one brand in five years, or

00:12:26.832 --> 00:12:30.564
a billion dollars in sales in
five years from this old factory.

00:12:30.564 --> 00:12:33.930
You will have 16 to 18 lines,

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:38.990
those lines are 400 to
600 cups per minute.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:43.063
They will cost anywhere 5 to $10 million,
and

00:12:43.063 --> 00:12:46.367
they will take about two years to build.

00:12:46.367 --> 00:12:50.996
So then in order to have them,
you make the calculations,

00:12:50.996 --> 00:12:54.781
in five years,
this is literally impossible.

00:12:54.781 --> 00:12:56.677
So if you're going to make it five years,

00:12:56.677 --> 00:12:59.124
you're probably going to
be 2 million cases a week.

00:12:59.124 --> 00:13:02.867
That means a pallet of
yogurt every 15 seconds.

00:13:02.867 --> 00:13:05.019
Now when you talk about it,

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:09.894
it's a quiet place there's
nobody there it's an old place.

00:13:11.726 --> 00:13:15.788
[SOUND] You can dismiss the idea and you
say you're not going to to be a billion

00:13:15.788 --> 00:13:19.999
dollars in sales in business in five
years and stay 100% independent.

00:13:19.999 --> 00:13:24.705
And you will find all the money
you needed to make this happen

00:13:24.705 --> 00:13:28.565
that you don't have to
raise penny of capital and

00:13:28.565 --> 00:13:32.915
then you will give the answer
to the world impossible.

00:13:32.915 --> 00:13:37.463
But then slowly start happening,
toilets turn into office,

00:13:37.463 --> 00:13:41.668
parking lot turns into a cooler
space with the trailers.

00:13:41.668 --> 00:13:44.378
You find used fillers somewhere else and

00:13:44.378 --> 00:13:47.416
people find it in their
nighttime talkings.

00:13:47.416 --> 00:13:51.045
And we literally turn that
place into that place, and

00:13:51.045 --> 00:13:53.876
I had no previous experience of doing so.

00:13:53.876 --> 00:13:59.211
So what I can tell you
in a short answer is,

00:13:59.211 --> 00:14:02.726
I found magic and purpose.

00:14:02.726 --> 00:14:08.981
And I found magic and
an amazing harmonation of love and anger.

00:14:08.981 --> 00:14:12.388
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's okay to be angry,

00:14:12.388 --> 00:14:14.485
I've been angry all my life.

00:14:14.485 --> 00:14:18.285
But if we can channel it
through in a very positive way,

00:14:18.285 --> 00:14:20.691
that becomes an enormous energy.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:26.637
And the result of it,
I can't put a price of the joy and

00:14:26.637 --> 00:14:29.260
benefit that you get from it.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.694
Is one of the most beautiful
thing that I've been part of,

00:14:32.694 --> 00:14:35.300
of that business effect on people's life?

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:41.380
&gt;&gt; Well, clearly, a lot of magic happened
in that old factory that you found,

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:45.731
a 95-year-old craft factory, and
you decided to take out a small

00:14:45.731 --> 00:14:47.165
business loan-
&gt;&gt; Yeah

00:14:47.165 --> 00:14:49.301
&gt;&gt; To purchase the factory to start

00:14:49.301 --> 00:14:50.888
producing the yogurt.

00:14:50.888 --> 00:14:56.050
At the GSB, we talk a lot about
venture capital and I'm curious about

00:14:56.050 --> 00:15:01.039
your decision to take a small
business loan versus raise VC money.

00:15:01.039 --> 00:15:07.481
&gt;&gt; Well, I didn't know about that world.

00:15:07.481 --> 00:15:11.217
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I swear I didn't know.

00:15:11.217 --> 00:15:14.677
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I saw this old factory and

00:15:14.677 --> 00:15:18.730
I started wondering how I can buy this.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.612
And there was this
KeyBank's local Johnston,

00:15:22.612 --> 00:15:28.728
New York KeyBank regional sales guys,
representatives, Pat Moochie and

00:15:28.728 --> 00:15:34.565
John Ryder, Pat Moochie passed away and
John Ryder is still out there.

00:15:34.565 --> 00:15:36.275
And I told them,
I want to do the right thing.

00:15:36.275 --> 00:15:39.041
John Ryder thought that was a crazy idea,
it's stupid.

00:15:39.041 --> 00:15:44.065
Pat Moochie this Sicilian Italian
guys most heavy big guy and

00:15:44.065 --> 00:15:45.946
says, I believe you.

00:15:48.251 --> 00:15:52.391
You gotta do something I don't know what
but you're going to do something I'm

00:15:52.391 --> 00:15:56.466
going to help you out and says, you have
this SBA loan if you can make a business

00:15:56.466 --> 00:16:01.603
plan And you come up with 40%
of the funding that needed.

00:16:01.603 --> 00:16:06.590
I'm sorry, if you can come up 10% of
the funding that needed, with the bank

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:11.597
will come up with 40%, and the SBA
will provide the 50% loan guarantee.

00:16:11.597 --> 00:16:14.982
So we can fund a million bucks,
we can buy this factory.

00:16:14.982 --> 00:16:19.619
So the factory was $700,000 and
I needed $300,000 to start it.

00:16:19.619 --> 00:16:23.961
So I wrote my first [LAUGH] business plan,
and basically,

00:16:23.961 --> 00:16:27.680
that's what they did for
me to buy that factory.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:33.034
Later on, I got calls from 2009, 2010.

00:16:33.034 --> 00:16:38.492
My fear was not that I want
to keep everything to myself.

00:16:38.492 --> 00:16:43.702
My fear was, if I bring somebody in,

00:16:43.702 --> 00:16:46.735
will they affect me?

00:16:46.735 --> 00:16:51.376
One, to crowd my head with unnecessary
noises and voices and talks, and

00:16:51.376 --> 00:16:52.911
all that kind of stuff.

00:16:52.911 --> 00:16:58.715
The second one is, will they force me
to do things that I don't want to do?

00:16:58.715 --> 00:17:04.516
So I try so hard to keep it simple,
keep it pure,

00:17:04.516 --> 00:17:11.783
and the business really
performed from 2007 October.

00:17:11.783 --> 00:17:17.289
And by 2009,
I kept the business so simple.

00:17:17.289 --> 00:17:25.072
I mean, I was in QuickBooks
until $670 million in sales.

00:17:25.072 --> 00:17:28.219
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not kidding.

00:17:28.219 --> 00:17:32.318
And then since they changed it,
I have no idea what the business is now.

00:17:32.318 --> 00:17:35.477
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I understood everything about business

00:17:35.477 --> 00:17:36.897
until $700 million.

00:17:36.897 --> 00:17:38.921
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's fair.

00:17:38.921 --> 00:17:43.071
&gt;&gt; But I kept it so simple and
I realized that, and

00:17:43.071 --> 00:17:48.180
I still give that fight at Chobani today,
it's there's

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:53.292
a lot of unnecessary noises
that happens in boardrooms,

00:17:53.292 --> 00:17:58.634
in factory floors, in titles,
and all that kind of stuff.

00:17:58.634 --> 00:18:01.561
So I kept it extremely simple and

00:18:01.561 --> 00:18:07.140
I'm still given that fight,
and I was very cost oriented.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:12.576
I was a factory worker, I did not
leave that factory for seven years,

00:18:12.576 --> 00:18:17.559
more than three days, maybe a month or
so for some sales calls.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:23.531
And I was so into every single details of
happening in the factory and the product,

00:18:23.531 --> 00:18:26.222
and every single detail
that you can think of.

00:18:26.222 --> 00:18:32.094
I mean, I was little crazy,
maybe I was a little bit too much.

00:18:32.094 --> 00:18:36.322
And there you would find opportunities,
problems about to happen, or

00:18:36.322 --> 00:18:38.657
solution that you need to come up with.

00:18:38.657 --> 00:18:44.197
And so that kept it really profitable,
so I didn't have to raise any capital.

00:18:44.197 --> 00:18:48.789
&gt;&gt; But you took a bet on yourself and
on your ability to bring Greek

00:18:48.789 --> 00:18:54.470
yogurt to the United States and
it obviously definitely paid off.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:56.837
But it's easy to forget that in 2010,

00:18:56.837 --> 00:19:00.072
there were no Greek yogurt
brands being advertised on TV.

00:19:00.072 --> 00:19:03.770
And then two years later,
there were eight different brands,

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:05.946
what did that competition do to you?

00:19:05.946 --> 00:19:10.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I knew it was coming, and
that's what I said to anybody who

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:15.411
come up with the idea of the food
items in the United States.

00:19:15.411 --> 00:19:18.681
Especially in the United States,
it's a very, very challenging environment.

00:19:18.681 --> 00:19:20.645
And I saw this very, very early on.

00:19:20.645 --> 00:19:24.293
So there's the separation
between specialty stores and

00:19:24.293 --> 00:19:26.728
the mass market in the United States.

00:19:26.728 --> 00:19:29.799
But if you go to let's say Turkey, Europe,

00:19:29.799 --> 00:19:34.115
any other countries there
are some middle market companies.

00:19:34.115 --> 00:19:36.036
There are regional businesses.

00:19:36.036 --> 00:19:39.144
There are businesses that are not so
big but not too small.

00:19:39.144 --> 00:19:42.178
They have capability to
supply regionally or cross.

00:19:42.178 --> 00:19:47.610
And those are almost don't
exist in this country,

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:53.559
if you're countrywide,
system wide mass food maker.

00:19:53.559 --> 00:20:00.660
So, early on, I really understood that
the big guys they never make innovations.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:02.155
They don't have to.
So when I started,

00:20:02.155 --> 00:20:05.640
you have a cup Dannon or
a cup of Yoplait at the time.

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:07.463
You look at it, 25 grams sugar.

00:20:07.463 --> 00:20:10.119
I mean, you have the ingredients
from top to bottom.

00:20:10.119 --> 00:20:11.929
I don't know if you guys
remember those days.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:15.996
And they were advertising all day
long how healthy this stuff is.

00:20:15.996 --> 00:20:19.699
And then the cost is extremely cheap.

00:20:19.699 --> 00:20:23.003
All the factories are made
to make those cups, and

00:20:23.003 --> 00:20:26.629
they have all the reasons to
keep that things that way.

00:20:26.629 --> 00:20:28.372
So you don't want anyone to disrupt that.

00:20:28.372 --> 00:20:33.286
And if you look at mass food environment
in this country, which is the reason of

00:20:33.286 --> 00:20:37.610
a lot of problems that we're facing today,
when it comes to health.

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:42.260
Is hold by large corporations,
large food corporations,

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:45.800
that have massive supply chain capability.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:50.272
And enormous amount of one to one
relationship with the retailers,

00:20:50.272 --> 00:20:55.058
that those are maybe 10,
maybe 12 companies that hold vast majority

00:20:55.058 --> 00:20:58.524
of food that produced and
consumed in this country.

00:20:58.524 --> 00:21:04.810
Because for startups, it's extremely
difficult to create those capabilities.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:09.685
So you can be a true partner with
Walmart or Kroger or Safeway.

00:21:09.685 --> 00:21:11.884
So what you become is a specialty player.

00:21:11.884 --> 00:21:15.090
So specialty like Whole Foods and
all those classes,

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.876
it's only maybe less
than 5% of the market.

00:21:17.876 --> 00:21:20.224
So 95% of the market is on the mass.

00:21:20.224 --> 00:21:23.805
So when somebody comes,
I have a great idea.

00:21:23.805 --> 00:21:27.959
Good, so you just worked so
hard for two years to create it and

00:21:27.959 --> 00:21:32.601
then you went knocked door and
door in all these specialty stores and

00:21:32.601 --> 00:21:35.160
prove that this works.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:38.602
And the big guys come for two questions,
one is do you want to sell?

00:21:38.602 --> 00:21:42.717
And sometimes you're forced to
sell because all the capital

00:21:42.717 --> 00:21:47.557
that you raised until that idea come
to life, and when it comes to food,

00:21:47.557 --> 00:21:49.910
it's very, very capital heavy.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:52.754
They come with questions,
you want to sell?

00:21:52.754 --> 00:21:53.765
Do you love what you do?

00:21:53.765 --> 00:21:56.030
Four time sales,
five times sales, whatever it is.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:59.876
But then they come to second half,
if you don't, I'll make the same thing.

00:21:59.876 --> 00:22:04.278
It's not your idea anymore,
it's everybody's idea, so

00:22:04.278 --> 00:22:06.840
it goes into a big guy's ability.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.729
Let's say oat milk came up, but
how many oat milk is out there, right?

00:22:09.729 --> 00:22:11.383
Everybody can make the oat milk now.

00:22:11.383 --> 00:22:16.426
So basically, when you have the supply
chain, you can be immune to competition,

00:22:16.426 --> 00:22:21.396
if your brand is very, very strong, and
your value proposition is very strong,

00:22:21.396 --> 00:22:24.048
and there's something unique about you.

00:22:24.048 --> 00:22:28.834
But if you don't have the supply chain,
you got no place to go.

00:22:28.834 --> 00:22:33.218
So what I said when I saw them I
said they're going to come, and

00:22:33.218 --> 00:22:36.741
before they come,
I have to make two decision.

00:22:36.741 --> 00:22:40.893
Either I want to stay small, Or

00:22:40.893 --> 00:22:43.657
I have to pass certain level that
they cannot hurt me anymore.

00:22:43.657 --> 00:22:46.519
And I bet on the second one.

00:22:46.519 --> 00:22:51.032
So at that time, they were so
slow, by the time they came up,

00:22:51.032 --> 00:22:54.319
I was already 6, $700 million sales.

00:22:54.319 --> 00:22:59.170
And I was distributed across the country,
and I was building a plant in Idaho.

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:02.502
But when they came, they came really,
really, really hard.

00:23:02.502 --> 00:23:07.611
Because you've just embarrassed one of
the largest yogurt makers in the world.

00:23:10.015 --> 00:23:15.015
In a very bad way, so they say
[LAUGH] I understand somebody come

00:23:15.015 --> 00:23:21.281
up with an idea and make some nice noises
in Whole Foods, in all those places.

00:23:21.281 --> 00:23:26.030
But now I'm losing shares in Stop &amp; Shop,
in Costco, this not nice.

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:31.477
So I always tell my colleagues,
they have an incubator,

00:23:31.477 --> 00:23:37.036
and say you have to focus on
manufacturing fundamentals,

00:23:37.036 --> 00:23:40.331
then you are in the food business.

00:23:40.331 --> 00:23:44.731
So at Chobani, people think that
I built a yogurt and flavors.

00:23:44.731 --> 00:23:48.961
90% of the time I spent,
its factories, supply chain,

00:23:48.961 --> 00:23:52.866
these boring details that
nobody wants to talk about.

00:23:52.866 --> 00:23:57.310
But in the end, if you're going to
disrupt any category in this country,

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:01.514
Which has not been disrupted in
a massive way if you look at it.

00:24:01.514 --> 00:24:04.009
You have to focus on the supply chain.

00:24:04.009 --> 00:24:08.217
&gt;&gt; Well, clearly you were able
to disrupt the the industry and

00:24:08.217 --> 00:24:13.940
rise above the competition, all the while
still focusing on the human spirit and

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:18.591
not just focusing on the spreadsheets,
as you've said before.

00:24:18.591 --> 00:24:23.212
And an incredible example of that,
of not just looking at the spreadsheets,

00:24:23.212 --> 00:24:26.982
is your decision to grant
shares to all 2,000 employees,

00:24:26.982 --> 00:24:29.631
including factory workers, in 2016.

00:24:29.631 --> 00:24:33.854
I'm sure there were some people who were
not thrilled with that decision, but

00:24:33.854 --> 00:24:34.943
you made it anyway.

00:24:34.943 --> 00:24:37.650
What was your thinking there?

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:42.470
&gt;&gt; I always said, and
it's still considered like as

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:45.925
if I'm the nice guy, I give a gift.

00:24:45.925 --> 00:24:50.742
And I have seen this unjust
from me growing up and

00:24:50.742 --> 00:24:56.644
becoming a young adult in university and
this in Turkey and

00:24:56.644 --> 00:25:01.591
later on here,
is responsibility of business.

00:25:01.591 --> 00:25:06.486
What I saw in that little town is that
factory was there for, like you said,

00:25:06.486 --> 00:25:07.819
80, 95 years.

00:25:07.819 --> 00:25:12.229
When the things were good,
the steam was coming off from the chimney,

00:25:12.229 --> 00:25:17.037
and the products were coming and the
trucks were coming and people had jobs.

00:25:17.037 --> 00:25:21.250
When things didn't look good,
somebody made a decision in

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:25.970
the headquarter that if we cut off
this business and you'll save so

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:29.109
much money, and those people left behind.

00:25:29.109 --> 00:25:34.158
So why is this factory workers,
and in this pandemic we saw,

00:25:34.158 --> 00:25:38.420
we call them frontline workers,
but who are they?

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:42.386
You had really truck drivers,
factory workers, nurses and

00:25:42.386 --> 00:25:45.652
all those people that,
some of them are parents,

00:25:45.652 --> 00:25:49.802
some of it are relatives,
that they move the society forward.

00:25:49.802 --> 00:25:54.962
But when the bad days comes in business,
they're the first one to be blamed.

00:25:54.962 --> 00:26:00.510
So in my scenario, 4 factory workers,
later on 10, later on 50,

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:05.325
later on 100 with me,
we built that things back together.

00:26:05.325 --> 00:26:09.744
I've never made that promise because I
didn't know if I could make that false

00:26:09.744 --> 00:26:10.363
promise.

00:26:10.363 --> 00:26:13.391
I didn't know if I would be succeed,
so I don't want to give some.

00:26:13.391 --> 00:26:18.544
But when I knew that we had it,
it was the decision I made day one

00:26:18.544 --> 00:26:24.395
that everyone who worked to build this
have right to have a share of it or

00:26:24.395 --> 00:26:28.090
part of the success
that we have generated.

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:33.272
And I truly believe in this new
way of looking at business and

00:26:33.272 --> 00:26:38.454
what it means in society where
it comes an enormous amount of

00:26:38.454 --> 00:26:45.628
strong effect on how society moves forward
and businesses are behaving that way.

00:26:45.628 --> 00:26:50.136
But this income inequality
between working people and

00:26:50.136 --> 00:26:55.539
people on top even in this country
is getting deeper and sharper.

00:26:55.539 --> 00:27:00.118
And that's the only way you can save
that is, governments can do policies and

00:27:00.118 --> 00:27:01.542
all that kind of stuff.

00:27:01.542 --> 00:27:08.035
But if we can make businesses to share
its success with the stakeholder and

00:27:08.035 --> 00:27:14.230
most importantly, if it is workers,
is the only way moving forward.

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:21.770
And I hope that this becomes a very normal
practice, not that you brag about it.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:26.390
It's the same way all the startups
that happen here in Silicon Valley,

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:28.244
it's a very common things.

00:27:28.244 --> 00:27:32.468
But it's not common when it comes to
the vast majority of service industry,

00:27:32.468 --> 00:27:35.970
manufacturing industry and
especially in our food industry.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:41.066
&gt;&gt; You've said before that social
impact needs to be a key part of

00:27:41.066 --> 00:27:46.561
a company's DNA and you clearly
represent that from the top down.

00:27:46.561 --> 00:27:51.497
But do you have some examples of how
that's represented from the bottom up?

00:27:52.966 --> 00:27:56.258
&gt;&gt; How it's represented from bottom up,
if you could give an example.

00:27:56.258 --> 00:28:01.030
&gt;&gt; So the kind of impact that
maybe a factory worker can

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:05.809
have on making social impact
part of the identity.

00:28:05.809 --> 00:28:12.062
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think

00:28:15.504 --> 00:28:18.042
There's this magic word, culture,

00:28:18.042 --> 00:28:22.572
and I've I've seen a lot of things
that people talk about culture.

00:28:22.572 --> 00:28:26.491
People talk about all this diversity,
inclusion,

00:28:26.491 --> 00:28:31.924
things that we offer, what's in
the handbook of employee handbooks and

00:28:31.924 --> 00:28:36.127
what do you write in the walls and
all that kind of stuff.

00:28:36.127 --> 00:28:41.808
A funny thing about culture is it's
either real or it's just not there,

00:28:41.808 --> 00:28:45.731
or it's just in the walls and
it's in the papers.

00:28:45.731 --> 00:28:53.479
When it's real, then everybody
is committed to be part of it.

00:28:53.479 --> 00:28:58.178
There is this unspoken rule that
starts happening in that environment.

00:28:58.178 --> 00:29:03.743
And for the founders, you'll be a founder,
a lot of people will be founder here,

00:29:03.743 --> 00:29:08.955
is that cultures are built mostly
in the very early beginning.

00:29:08.955 --> 00:29:11.565
So I'd say the first few years, and

00:29:11.565 --> 00:29:16.873
what happens in the first few years is
you are very intensely together and

00:29:16.873 --> 00:29:21.589
it's very close, and
there's a lot of conversation happens.

00:29:21.589 --> 00:29:26.641
And you face a lot of challenges,
and you find a lot of solutions, and

00:29:26.641 --> 00:29:31.804
then you kind of come too close to
almost die, finish the whole thing.

00:29:31.804 --> 00:29:39.333
In those environments, the real you come
out, how you behave in those environments.

00:29:39.333 --> 00:29:43.265
There's a recording is
happening during that time and

00:29:43.265 --> 00:29:46.435
those stories being told later on forever.

00:29:46.435 --> 00:29:53.010
So what I can say is my early
few years were so intense, and

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:58.366
I was so present, and
I'm so proud of myself.

00:29:58.366 --> 00:29:58.999
I really am.

00:29:58.999 --> 00:30:01.346
I'm not proud of a lot
of things I've done, but

00:30:01.346 --> 00:30:04.297
I'm really proud of the first
few years that I was in there.

00:30:04.297 --> 00:30:08.078
I was in a meeting in the second or

00:30:08.078 --> 00:30:13.132
third year and I gather all our employees.

00:30:13.132 --> 00:30:16.774
And these are all upstate New York,

00:30:16.774 --> 00:30:21.154
Harley drivers, big guys, some of them.

00:30:21.154 --> 00:30:24.328
And I brought them all in
the cafeteria and I said,

00:30:24.328 --> 00:30:29.317
we just started something magical,
I can see this is going somewhere really,

00:30:29.317 --> 00:30:32.657
really good, but
I'm not sure if I can handle this.

00:30:32.657 --> 00:30:35.033
I have never been part of this before.

00:30:35.033 --> 00:30:36.885
So I said I don't have anyone around me.

00:30:36.885 --> 00:30:37.884
I don't have a mother.

00:30:37.884 --> 00:30:38.609
I don't have wife.

00:30:38.609 --> 00:30:39.222
I don't have girlfriend.

00:30:39.222 --> 00:30:40.269
I don't have children.

00:30:40.269 --> 00:30:43.370
I don't have family members, I'm alone.

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:46.044
So I have you, you're my family.

00:30:46.044 --> 00:30:52.078
And if you see me I go in a wrong way,
if you see me, I act in a wrong way,

00:30:52.078 --> 00:30:57.285
like nose up in the air,
start behaving like I'm a big guy,

00:30:57.285 --> 00:31:00.647
big shot and start doing weird stuff.

00:31:00.647 --> 00:31:03.532
I give permission every single
one of you to hit me on the face.

00:31:03.532 --> 00:31:06.266
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I literally said that and

00:31:06.266 --> 00:31:07.284
there are some big guys.

00:31:07.284 --> 00:31:09.095
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I said,

00:31:09.095 --> 00:31:14.603
you would give me a service if you do that
because I don't want this magic to done,

00:31:14.603 --> 00:31:18.743
and I'm not sure if I can handle it,
I'm just a human being.

00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:22.285
And nobody hit me.

00:31:22.285 --> 00:31:22.991
&gt;&gt; Yet.

00:31:22.991 --> 00:31:25.936
&gt;&gt; But-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:31:25.936 --> 00:31:30.692
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I think if I go

00:31:30.692 --> 00:31:36.044
back there is Maria,

00:31:36.044 --> 00:31:39.908
there is Rick,

00:31:39.908 --> 00:31:44.070
there's Mike,

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:48.541
there's Frank.

00:31:48.541 --> 00:31:54.410
I can name a lot of people in
that place that has significant,

00:31:54.410 --> 00:31:59.140
Kyle, significant Impact on
Chobani as much as I am, but

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:02.875
their names are not
mentioned in newspapers and

00:32:02.875 --> 00:32:05.972
they're not sitting here next to you.

00:32:05.972 --> 00:32:10.243
So what I'm saying is,
if you create that culture,

00:32:10.243 --> 00:32:15.110
every single individual
contributes as big as what you do.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:18.606
&gt;&gt; Well, that's great and
you've shared their names here today and

00:32:18.606 --> 00:32:20.737
I'm sure they really appreciate that.

00:32:20.737 --> 00:32:25.060
Another area you've had amazing
impact is with refugees.

00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:30.517
And 30% of Chobani's manufacturing
force are refugees or immigrants.

00:32:30.517 --> 00:32:35.075
Unfortunately, not everyone is as
welcoming of these communities as you are.

00:32:35.075 --> 00:32:40.068
So, could you maybe share a statistic or
an argument that you have found to be most

00:32:40.068 --> 00:32:43.528
effective in convincing
people to change their minds?

00:32:43.528 --> 00:32:45.281
&gt;&gt; I'm so happy you brought this up.

00:32:45.281 --> 00:32:51.807
So, Tent came out of this experience
that I had in Upstate New York,

00:32:51.807 --> 00:32:58.690
when we expand the factory, and
hire everybody that was let go before.

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:03.586
Expanded a little bit further and then hit
a town in Utica, which I used to live and

00:33:03.586 --> 00:33:06.903
people said there are refugees
that settled in Utica.

00:33:06.903 --> 00:33:11.747
And this is 2008, 2009 that area from
all across the world legally being

00:33:11.747 --> 00:33:15.963
settled as refugees they're having
very much hard time to find jobs.

00:33:15.963 --> 00:33:22.028
So, I went to the refugee agency,
and I said what's the problem?

00:33:22.028 --> 00:33:25.475
Actually this heavenly lady, she said,

00:33:25.475 --> 00:33:30.202
they don't speak the language,
they don't have cars,

00:33:30.202 --> 00:33:35.228
they don't have trainings of
jobs that are available here.

00:33:35.228 --> 00:33:39.012
But most importantly people really are,
little,

00:33:39.012 --> 00:33:42.098
I said what, afraid of them or what is it?

00:33:42.098 --> 00:33:45.871
People are not familiar with people coming
from Asia and Africa and Middle East,

00:33:45.871 --> 00:33:47.161
this is a rural community.

00:33:47.161 --> 00:33:49.676
I said, okay, I need workers.

00:33:49.676 --> 00:33:54.488
I had everyone in my community that
is available, let's do something.

00:33:54.488 --> 00:33:57.234
So we'll get some cars and buses,

00:33:57.234 --> 00:34:01.505
we'll get some translators and
we see how that goes.

00:34:01.505 --> 00:34:02.381
He said, are you sure?

00:34:02.381 --> 00:34:05.195
I said, yeah, I think this will be good.

00:34:05.195 --> 00:34:09.959
So, this started, not a refugee
work it was a community work.

00:34:09.959 --> 00:34:13.086
Where people who are left behind,
left out,

00:34:13.086 --> 00:34:17.793
and they have no way of getting in and
just breaking those barriers.

00:34:17.793 --> 00:34:21.603
But just cars, translators,
and in job training,

00:34:21.603 --> 00:34:24.815
it's an HR work really
it's not a big deal.

00:34:24.815 --> 00:34:28.799
Just bring them in and
let people get to know each other and

00:34:28.799 --> 00:34:30.721
interact with each other.

00:34:30.721 --> 00:34:34.021
And it became this thing and it brought so

00:34:34.021 --> 00:34:37.817
much energy to the company,
people loved it.

00:34:37.817 --> 00:34:42.393
Brotherhood, sisterhood is built and
people have never had seen someone from

00:34:42.393 --> 00:34:46.083
Nepal or someone from Ethiopia or
someone from South America.

00:34:46.083 --> 00:34:50.933
And it was so magical, I just saw things
are happening, I couldn't believe my eyes.

00:34:50.933 --> 00:34:54.886
So when I built a plant in Idaho,
did the same thing.

00:34:54.886 --> 00:34:58.807
And then, we saw three years later,
four years later,

00:34:58.807 --> 00:35:02.898
and people will come to me tell
me what their experiences,

00:35:02.898 --> 00:35:06.500
I just bought home,
my daughter is in this school.

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:09.394
So, this magic of having
an access to job for

00:35:09.394 --> 00:35:13.015
a refugee is the moment that
they stop being a refugee.

00:35:13.015 --> 00:35:17.400
That is the only moment they start being
part of a community, they start being part

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:21.676
of a society, they can build their life
for themselves and for their children.

00:35:21.676 --> 00:35:27.029
So I thought, how could I take this
experience that I had into a large scale.

00:35:27.029 --> 00:35:32.962
So, I went to Geneva and talk to UNHCR and
IRC and some other things.

00:35:32.962 --> 00:35:34.793
And what I'd realized,

00:35:34.793 --> 00:35:39.849
the absence of business in this
refugee topic, which is probably one

00:35:39.849 --> 00:35:44.923
of the most alarming humanitarian
crisis that we're facing today.

00:35:44.923 --> 00:35:50.380
We have faced this during World War II,
when Europe had so much refugees,

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:54.958
especially Jewish refugees
are fleeing all over the world and

00:35:54.958 --> 00:35:58.233
humanity did not perform
well at that time.

00:35:58.233 --> 00:36:02.227
And this is again happening, and
it's happening other places.

00:36:02.227 --> 00:36:07.806
And I thought the other
saddest thing is how

00:36:07.806 --> 00:36:15.091
this topic is being part of
political conversations and

00:36:15.091 --> 00:36:20.518
how it's profiled so unfairly, so bad.

00:36:20.518 --> 00:36:25.154
And the only way you can cut this through
is if you can tie it into power of

00:36:25.154 --> 00:36:29.555
brands and businesses and CEOs,
which today have more respect and

00:36:29.555 --> 00:36:32.318
believability rates than anybody else.

00:36:32.318 --> 00:36:34.785
So I launched it with Airbnb,

00:36:34.785 --> 00:36:39.929
Joe has been amazing on this topic,
Airbnb has been amazing.

00:36:39.929 --> 00:36:45.514
Mastercard, IG has been
amazing on this from very

00:36:45.514 --> 00:36:50.576
early on and few others in Davos in 2016.

00:36:50.576 --> 00:36:55.504
And what I said is,
we'll do scientific studies.

00:36:55.504 --> 00:36:59.648
We've done a lot with Stern,
business schools, and some economists, and

00:36:59.648 --> 00:37:00.699
branding people.

00:37:00.699 --> 00:37:05.660
We did market research in each markets,
Europe, South America,

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:08.061
US, Canada and demographics.

00:37:08.061 --> 00:37:13.089
And we said here, statistics are,
refugees are staying

00:37:13.089 --> 00:37:17.613
in the job 30 to 40%
longer than anybody else.

00:37:17.613 --> 00:37:22.179
Productivity level, and
we have done all these studies,

00:37:22.179 --> 00:37:25.356
is twice more than anyone you will have.

00:37:25.356 --> 00:37:29.826
And then, every dollar you spend
on refugees in five years,

00:37:29.826 --> 00:37:33.771
the longest,
usually it's a lot earlier than that,

00:37:33.771 --> 00:37:39.930
the longest in any society in five years
pays back and then positive after forever.

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:44.448
All these studies we've done, and
then be the marketing studies that say,

00:37:44.448 --> 00:37:48.895
how people think about your brand and
your company, if you hire refugees,

00:37:48.895 --> 00:37:51.596
train refugees, and you're pro refugees.

00:37:51.596 --> 00:37:57.216
And we did all these market studies and
found that what it does to brand and

00:37:57.216 --> 00:38:00.812
what it does to talent
coming into your company

00:38:00.812 --> 00:38:05.629
when they know you're socially
active in the refugee field.

00:38:05.629 --> 00:38:09.602
So one by one we convince
companies to come and join.

00:38:09.602 --> 00:38:12.883
Today we are over 200 companies,
worldwide.

00:38:12.883 --> 00:38:15.777
And when the Afghanis were coming,

00:38:15.777 --> 00:38:19.453
refugees from Afghanistan
just a month ago.

00:38:19.453 --> 00:38:24.339
We had over 60 companies signed
up publicly, very beautifully and

00:38:24.339 --> 00:38:28.055
committing to hire refugees
train them right away.

00:38:28.055 --> 00:38:33.711
So this coalition that we have built
together, it became extremely powerful.

00:38:33.711 --> 00:38:36.198
It's the first business
coalition has been built.

00:38:36.198 --> 00:38:41.493
Now, if you talk to anybody,
we've been to Colombia,

00:38:41.493 --> 00:38:48.380
we've been to Canada, we've been to
refugee camps all over the world.

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:51.812
If you talk to the companies who
have gone into this business,

00:38:51.812 --> 00:38:56.369
there's this enormous amount of effect
internally in the company that you have.

00:38:56.369 --> 00:38:59.738
So it's really, really good for business.

00:38:59.738 --> 00:39:05.617
And I don't find any other way other than
businesses coming into this topics and

00:39:05.617 --> 00:39:08.699
any other topics that society is facing.

00:39:08.699 --> 00:39:10.733
And make it part of the HR department,

00:39:10.733 --> 00:39:14.941
part of their manufacturing and
people department, or service department.

00:39:14.941 --> 00:39:19.982
And it's not check the box,
it's organically imbedded into operation,

00:39:19.982 --> 00:39:22.952
that you can see the light
under the tunnel.

00:39:22.952 --> 00:39:26.958
But it's very early beginning,
I am extremely passionate about it.

00:39:26.958 --> 00:39:31.875
And I think these people have
gone through so much pain and

00:39:31.875 --> 00:39:36.899
sorrow and difficulties that
they didn't want to leave,

00:39:36.899 --> 00:39:40.193
they didn't want to go through this.

00:39:40.193 --> 00:39:44.550
But now, they are in these un-stuck
places that human capacity and

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:48.224
potential through business
they can come into society,

00:39:48.224 --> 00:39:51.681
they can come into their life and
rebuild their life.

00:39:51.681 --> 00:39:55.187
And the businesses can benefit and
society can benefit from this.

00:39:55.187 --> 00:39:58.848
So it's very simple,
hire them, Train them, and

00:39:58.848 --> 00:40:04.266
use your supply chain to keep
them in wherever they are into

00:40:04.266 --> 00:40:08.921
the supply chain, and
you'll benefit greatly.

00:40:08.921 --> 00:40:12.972
&gt;&gt; I love that your answer has
statistics to back you up.

00:40:12.972 --> 00:40:13.548
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:40:13.548 --> 00:40:16.646
&gt;&gt; But of course as always,
you're speaking from the heart and

00:40:16.646 --> 00:40:18.549
focusing on the human spirit there.

00:40:18.549 --> 00:40:23.474
And our theme for this slate of speakers
this year has been going beyond,

00:40:23.474 --> 00:40:26.716
going beyond expectations of a CEO or
a leader.

00:40:26.716 --> 00:40:31.603
And I think you've done an incredible
job of exemplifying that, so thank you.

00:40:31.603 --> 00:40:33.078
&gt;&gt; Thank you and,

00:40:33.078 --> 00:40:41.857
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:40:41.857 --> 00:40:45.412
&gt;&gt; I'm so honored to be with you and

00:40:45.412 --> 00:40:47.000
with you all.

