questions from the audience.

00:41:38.629 --> 00:41:41.837
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for
being here and inspiring us.

00:41:41.837 --> 00:41:48.322
I love the comment about the joy, and
creating a culture from the beginning.

00:41:48.322 --> 00:41:51.855
So my question to you is,
Chobani's doing so

00:41:51.855 --> 00:41:56.231
much in trying to go forefront in so
many so many topics.

00:41:56.231 --> 00:41:59.815
And, there are two things that capture
my attention when looking at what

00:41:59.815 --> 00:42:00.710
they're doing.

00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:05.730
One is, this incubator for new businesses
and when I first read I was wow,

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:10.452
they're actually funding other
companies who could be competition.

00:42:10.452 --> 00:42:14.028
[LAUGH] So I wanted to ask you
a little bit about that initiative of

00:42:14.028 --> 00:42:14.985
the incubator.

00:42:14.985 --> 00:42:19.599
And also what would you like to see
more in the sector to help, move and

00:42:19.599 --> 00:42:21.761
push this initiative forward?

00:42:21.761 --> 00:42:27.473
&gt;&gt; What a beautiful question,
I find food in the US is one of

00:42:27.473 --> 00:42:32.596
the fundamentals a problem
that we need to solve.

00:42:32.596 --> 00:42:37.231
And, the problem is you can make food
that is very good ingredients, but

00:42:37.231 --> 00:42:40.277
it's very expensive
people cannot afford it.

00:42:40.277 --> 00:42:44.990
And and then the big guys they make
very bad food is very accessible.

00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:49.143
So how do you make accessible food that
is wholesomely made, is nutrition made?

00:42:49.143 --> 00:42:52.537
That every children every family
members across the country can have it.

00:42:52.537 --> 00:42:56.998
So that was my thing is good food for
all, delicious,

00:42:56.998 --> 00:43:00.383
nutritious, natural and accessible.

00:43:00.383 --> 00:43:06.090
Because you can make $5 yogurt but,
how many people are going to buy that?

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:10.223
So, what I did is we found a way,

00:43:10.223 --> 00:43:14.828
we found a way to make $1 a yogurt.

00:43:14.828 --> 00:43:20.387
That made extremely wholesome
ingredients that have 11 gram protein.

00:43:20.387 --> 00:43:26.734
That available in Walmart for $1 Kroger
for $1 20 that everybody can buy.

00:43:26.734 --> 00:43:30.955
So I wanted to share that
with the startups, so

00:43:30.955 --> 00:43:35.302
I started the incubator,
Chobani Incubator.

00:43:35.302 --> 00:43:38.556
And I said no strings attached
I don't want anything back,

00:43:38.556 --> 00:43:40.912
I don't want any percentage or anything.

00:43:40.912 --> 00:43:45.530
All I want to do is,
if I was starting my journey in 2007,

00:43:45.530 --> 00:43:50.525
the things that I wish I had known
back then, what would those be?

00:43:50.525 --> 00:43:54.835
And I made a list of five,
kind of five or seven.

00:43:54.835 --> 00:43:58.968
And I wanted to share those with people
who are starting their journey that had

00:43:58.968 --> 00:44:00.758
the like minded entrepreneurs.

00:44:00.758 --> 00:44:03.906
They have the right attitude,
and they see the same thing.

00:44:03.906 --> 00:44:07.227
And we started this it became
very very popular, and

00:44:07.227 --> 00:44:11.544
incubator people will come four months,
five months stay with us.

00:44:11.544 --> 00:44:15.155
Engineering, quality,
plants, branding, marketing,

00:44:15.155 --> 00:44:17.432
all the doors are open for all of them.

00:44:17.432 --> 00:44:22.330
And we had amazing women founders came,

00:44:22.330 --> 00:44:25.506
veterans founders came.

00:44:25.506 --> 00:44:29.314
And the farmers and intrapreneurs came and

00:44:29.314 --> 00:44:33.348
I started with 32,
I did it for four years.

00:44:33.348 --> 00:44:37.944
I brought 30 students from Turkey
from all across the country and

00:44:37.944 --> 00:44:40.584
then five, six startups every year.

00:44:40.584 --> 00:44:45.250
That was the summer program, I think one
of the most beautiful thing we can do is

00:44:45.250 --> 00:44:49.725
share the experience and knowledge,
without being any string attached.

00:44:49.725 --> 00:44:53.170
And opening up so
the second reason I had is,

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:56.815
we cannot be alone on
this we need a community.

00:44:56.815 --> 00:45:01.452
So that's the reason, either another
yogurt maker, another food maker,

00:45:01.452 --> 00:45:02.474
whatever it is.

00:45:02.474 --> 00:45:07.964
This new good food making movement for
all, has to be a communities for

00:45:07.964 --> 00:45:11.705
us to be able to make a dent,
into this problem.

00:45:11.705 --> 00:45:15.868
So I thought, we might not be
as big as the other big guys.

00:45:15.868 --> 00:45:19.784
But if we are part of a community and
we collaborate with each other,

00:45:19.784 --> 00:45:23.717
we share from each other,
then we can really make this a mass thing.

00:45:23.717 --> 00:45:28.060
So that was the second reason
that I started it, yeah.

00:45:30.547 --> 00:45:34.679
&gt;&gt; Hi Hamdi this is Mitad from Istanbul,
I'm also a fan [CROSSTALK] fan.

00:45:34.679 --> 00:45:37.568
So, thank you so much for coming here and

00:45:37.568 --> 00:45:41.909
sharing your insights about
entrepreneurship and impact.

00:45:41.909 --> 00:45:46.699
And I would like to your comments
on being an international

00:45:46.699 --> 00:45:51.216
immigrant entrepreneur in
the US was very important.

00:45:51.216 --> 00:45:54.000
And was very, truly inspiring for

00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:59.484
all the international students who
want to start businesses here.

00:45:59.484 --> 00:46:03.659
And I would like to ask what
would be your top three tips for

00:46:03.659 --> 00:46:06.712
them to start great businesses in the US?

00:46:06.712 --> 00:46:14.013
&gt;&gt; What a great question with that,
Really get to know America.

00:46:15.926 --> 00:46:19.307
And, what I can suggest for

00:46:19.307 --> 00:46:23.872
people like us coming from outside.

00:46:23.872 --> 00:46:27.667
For the Americans, there's politics,
there is celebrity, there is footballs and

00:46:27.667 --> 00:46:30.004
all that kind of stuff,
that's a different thing.

00:46:30.004 --> 00:46:35.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But for us, we come with pre-judgments,

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:41.543
and those judgments can be
because of what we read.

00:46:41.543 --> 00:46:45.986
Because of the political background
we come from, whatever that might be.

00:46:45.986 --> 00:46:51.685
And I tell this to refugees to
get rid of all those pre-ideas,

00:46:51.685 --> 00:46:56.850
get to know America,
if this is what you want to live in.

00:46:56.850 --> 00:47:01.745
And I say that because dismissal is
extremely easy like I don't like this,

00:47:01.745 --> 00:47:05.952
I don't like what they do here,
I don't like what they do here.

00:47:05.952 --> 00:47:10.708
But if you look at it for
the large part, we are the same, right?

00:47:10.708 --> 00:47:15.703
So, when I went upstate
New York walked in that farm,

00:47:15.703 --> 00:47:23.029
in New York City I couldn't there's more
Turks in New York City than natives.

00:47:23.029 --> 00:47:25.442
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:47:25.442 --> 00:47:27.956
it's Turkey in New York City
if you want to stay in Turkey.

00:47:27.956 --> 00:47:33.878
But, when I was upstate New York, these
farmers the people have been there for

00:47:33.878 --> 00:47:36.764
generations you get to know the soil.

00:47:36.764 --> 00:47:39.749
You get to know the culture,
you get to know the background.

00:47:39.749 --> 00:47:46.175
And start loving apple pie,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:47:46.175 --> 00:47:46.689
&gt;&gt; And,

00:47:46.689 --> 00:47:52.034
Thanksgiving the magic of Thanksgiving,
I was amazed by it.

00:47:52.034 --> 00:47:57.072
Interactions of the farmers with
each other, I was amazed by it.

00:47:57.072 --> 00:48:00.469
And, And I celebrated those a lot.

00:48:00.469 --> 00:48:05.037
And when I start celebrating then I
started to get to know the people and

00:48:05.037 --> 00:48:06.980
the culture and the history.

00:48:06.980 --> 00:48:09.828
And I think in the entrepreneurship,

00:48:09.828 --> 00:48:14.814
there's a little bit of anthropology,
is really involved in it.

00:48:14.814 --> 00:48:18.386
And that is your own
experience with people,

00:48:18.386 --> 00:48:21.680
without the judgement, to understand.

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:27.164
And people look at Chobani they think
we are very liberal activist brand.

00:48:27.164 --> 00:48:31.005
You have to understand, our two plans
in up state New York, and Idaho,

00:48:31.005 --> 00:48:33.453
they're are 70% red states, red areas.

00:48:33.453 --> 00:48:38.994
And these are one of my closest brothers,
and sisters.

00:48:38.994 --> 00:48:42.386
We made human connections because
we understood each other.

00:48:42.386 --> 00:48:50.803
I still make my argument that there should
be minimum wage with the Idaho government.

00:48:50.803 --> 00:48:55.995
I still make an argument that when
they ban transgenders in schools and

00:48:55.995 --> 00:48:58.378
I strongly disagree with that.

00:48:58.378 --> 00:49:02.928
But in a human level we understand each
other we can sit down and break bread and

00:49:02.928 --> 00:49:04.125
talk about things.

00:49:04.125 --> 00:49:08.258
And I can still celebrate the deep
culture of Idaho in a feminine level and

00:49:08.258 --> 00:49:09.628
in other other levels.

00:49:09.628 --> 00:49:13.354
So what one of my recommendation
is truly try to understand, and

00:49:13.354 --> 00:49:16.253
you'll find more beauty
than you ever imagined.

00:49:16.253 --> 00:49:20.820
Especially in the rural areas,
especially in the family interactions,

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:22.891
is there some not so good things?

00:49:22.891 --> 00:49:24.799
Of course, it's everywhere.

00:49:24.799 --> 00:49:30.570
And the second one is,
don't try to be like them.

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:35.816
Stay who you are, what you brought
where you come from are so beautiful,

00:49:35.816 --> 00:49:41.675
so amazing and so valuable, and this
country celebrates that in tremendously.

00:49:41.675 --> 00:49:44.598
So how do you make sure that what you see,
and

00:49:44.598 --> 00:49:48.946
what you bring that can beautifully
homogenize with each other?

00:49:48.946 --> 00:49:52.294
I never forget being a shepherd
son from eastern part of Turkey,

00:49:52.294 --> 00:49:53.600
I never forget that boy.

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:57.490
And if I go back tomorrow and if I put
a mask on and nobody recognize me,

00:49:57.490 --> 00:50:01.045
I can go to that mountain, and
start being under that tent, and

00:50:01.045 --> 00:50:04.156
be with the shepherds and
they would never know I left.

00:50:06.200 --> 00:50:09.382
Because I never forgot that I
live that every single day.

00:50:09.382 --> 00:50:14.927
So you don't have to leave who you are to
become successful for another place.

00:50:14.927 --> 00:50:19.913
The most successful person is brings
the beauty of where you come from,

00:50:19.913 --> 00:50:22.415
and to take and homogenize it here.

00:50:22.415 --> 00:50:25.565
And I always said I brought
a seed from but I come and

00:50:25.565 --> 00:50:29.541
I found the beautiful soil here and
that's become this plant.

00:50:29.541 --> 00:50:31.097
&gt;&gt; It's beautiful.

00:50:31.097 --> 00:50:40.774
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

